## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design scalable, policy-aware prompt orchestration with strong privacy guards and precise latency constraints, reflecting real-world multi-tenant systems at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy binding
- Guardrails to prevent system prompt leakage
- Modular routing to multiple tools
- Determinism testing under latency budgets
- Structured audit for compliance and debugging

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal routing prototype (conceptual)
function routePrompt(prompt, tenant, policyStore){
  const policy = policyStore[tenant];
  const guarded = applyGuard(prompt, policy);
  return { plan: ["SQL-builder","DataMasker","SummaryFormatter"], guarded };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate non-deterministic outputs across model variants under tight latency? 
- How would you scale tenant policies and audits across many tenants?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to translate policy into a concrete routing mechanism with auditability for multi-tenant enterprise chat systems.

## Key Concepts

- Signals: data-sensitivity, intent, policy-violations
- Modes: taboo-free, context-constrained, escalate
- Validation: unit tests with synthetic prompts and edge cases

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype
function analyze(p){
  return { policyViolation: false, redacted: false };
}
function routePrompt(p){
  const s = analyze(p);
  if (s.policyViolation) return { mode: 'escalate', reason: 'policy' };
  if (s.redacted) return { mode: 'context-constrained', reason: 'redaction' };
  return { mode: 'taboo-free' };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you log decisions for audits without leaking PII?
- How would you detect drift in routing quality over time?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses the ability to design intelligent, cost-aware routing systems for multi-module AI architectures in real-time environments, requiring consideration of performance trade-offs, safety constraints, and practical implementation details.

## Key Concepts

- Latency budgeting and real-time performance optimization
- Cost-aware routing and resource allocation
- Safety gating and data sensitivity handling
- Modular prompt orchestration and fallback mechanisms
- Policy-based decision making with precedence rules

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(prompt, signals) {
  const { budget, latency, safety, data_sensitivity } = signals;

  // Safety-first routing
  if (data_sensitivity > 0.7 || safety > 0.5) {
    return {
      module: 'SafetyGuard',
      plan: ['analyze_content', 'apply_filters', 'return_sanitized'],
      payload: { prompt, safety_score: safety, sensitivity_level: data_sensitivity }
    };
  }

  // Cost-optimized routing
  if (budget < 0.3 || latency < 100) {
    return {
      module: 'LightSummarizer',
      plan: ['extract_key_points', 'generate_summary'],
      payload: { prompt, max_tokens: 150 }
    };
  }

  // Default to comprehensive processing
  return {
    module: 'CitationEmbedder',
    plan: ['analyze_content', 'extract_citations', 'generate_embeddings'],
    payload: { prompt, include_sources: true }
  };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to design pragmatic tooling that ensures prompt reliability without requiring complex NLP infrastructure, focusing on version control and output consistency.

## Key Concepts
- Semantic prompt versioning and management
- Output determinism validation through stable hashing
- Delta scoring for output comparison
- Lightweight testing frameworks
- Performance observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype showing two prompts and hash comparison
const crypto = require('crypto');

function hash(obj) {
  return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(JSON.stringify(obj)).digest('hex');
}

function calculateDelta(output1, output2) {
  const hash1 = hash(output1);
  const hash2 = hash(output2);
  return hash1 === hash2 ? 0 : 1;
}

const prompts = {
  v1: 'Get monthly signup counts by region',
  v2: 'Calculate monthly user signups organized by geographic region'
};

function mockLLM(prompt) {
  return { data: `Response for: ${prompt}`, timestamp: Date.now() };
}

function selectVersion() {
  const outputs = {
    v1: mockLLM(prompts.v1),
    v2: mockLLM(prompts.v2)
  };

  const delta = calculateDelta(outputs.v1, outputs.v2);
  const selectedVersion = delta === 0 ? 'v1' : 'v2';

  return {
    selectedVersion,
    payload: outputs[selectedVersion],
    deltaScore: delta
  };
}

console.log(selectVersion());
```

## Implementation Notes
The system uses SHA-256 hashing for deterministic output comparison and a simple delta score to measure consistency between versions.

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to enforce tenancy isolation and data minimization in prompts and outputs, plus post-checks before delivery.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-augmented prompts, tenancy scoping, redaction, post-filtering, test harness concepts.

## Code Example
```javascript
function wrapPrompt(tenantId, userPrompt) {
  // basic tenant-scoped redaction and prefix
  const safePrompt = `[TENANT:${tenantId}] PROMPT: ${userPrompt}`;
  return safePrompt;
}

function mockLLM(prompt){
  // simulate a model that may leak data
  return `Response for ${prompt} with tenant id ${prompt.match(/TENANT:(\\w+)/)[1]}`;
}

function postCheck(response, tenantId){
  // naive leakage check
  if (response.includes(tenantId)) return null;
  return response;
}

console.log(postCheck(mockLLM(wrapPrompt('T123','List logs for user emails')),
  'T123'));
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you evolve this for real-world tenancy boundaries and performance at scale?
- What tests would you add to catch redaction misses and leakage?
- How would you handle dynamic redaction rules per tenant?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design runtime policy enforcement and safe prompt routing under latency and drift.

## Key Concepts
- Runtime policy loading
- Injection detection and redaction
- Token budgeting and trimming
- Auditable decision logs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
async function buildPrompt(basePrompt){
  const policy = await fetch('http://localhost:8000/policy').then(r=>r.json());
  // injection guard
  let p = basePrompt.replace(/{{.*?}}/g, '');
  // secrets redaction
  p = p.replace(/(API_KEY|SECRET|PASSWORD)=[^\s]+/g, '[REDACTED]');
  // token cap (pseudo)
  const max = policy.maxTokens ?? 512; 
  return p.length > max ? p.slice(0, max) : p;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test resilience to policy drift over time?
- What instrumentation would you add to monitor latency and false positives in production?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to enforce data policies in prompt processing, not just theory. It probes edge-case handling and testability.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt safety layering
- Data_sensitivity tagging
- Policy-driven guard rails and auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal guard example
function guardPrompt(prompt, policy){
  const intent = parseIntent(prompt);
  const data = tagSensitivity(prompt, policy);
  if(!allowed(data, policy)){ throw new Error('Policy violation'); }
  const guarded = applyGuard(prompt, policy);
  const check = `compliant:${Boolean(guarded)}`;
  return {guardedPrompt: guarded, check};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model conflicting signals (privacy vs analytics) and resolve them?
- How would you test for prompt leakage across multi-hop prompts?
